  <section class="section section--alt" aria-labelledby="status-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 id="status-heading" class="sr-only">Results status summary</h2>
      <div class="status-grid">
        <div class="status-card">
          <h3>Dry Lab</h3>
          <div class="status-items">
            <div class="status-item"><span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span> Structure prediction &amp; validation (both domains)</div>
            <div class="status-item"><span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span> Cex cellobiose-docking study</div>
            <div class="status-item"><span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span> Mutation-candidate screening, Cex &amp; CenA</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="status-card">
          <h3>Wet Lab</h3>
          <div class="status-items">
            <div class="status-item"><span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span> Cloning, library generation, sequencing</div>
            <div class="status-item"><span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span> Congo red + X-gluc screens (30 mutants)</div>
            <div class="status-item"><span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span> Quantitative MUC assay, validated &amp; applied</div>
            <div class="status-item"><span class="badge badge--neutral">No improvement confirmed</span> No mutant beat wild type past the noise floor</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- ═══ DRY LAB RESULTS ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <section class="section" id="drylab" aria-labelledby="drylab-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-header">
        <p class="eyebrow">Computational results</p>
        <h2 id="drylab-heading">Dry Lab — where should directed evolution even look?</h2>
        <p>
          Before touching a single bacterium, we used structure prediction and docking software
          to make an educated guess at which parts of CxnA were worth targeting — and, just as
          importantly, which predictions we should and shouldn't trust.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── DL-R1: Structure Prediction ─── -->
      <div class="result-block" id="drylab-structure">
        <div class="result-header">
          <div class="result-id">DL-R1</div>
          <div>
            <h3>What does CxnA actually look like?</h3>
            <div class="result-meta">
              <span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span>
              <span class="result-method">Methods: <a href={`${b}/methods#drylab`}>DL-1</a></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-body">
          <p>
            CxnA has no solved crystal structure of its own, so we predicted one with
            ColabFold/AlphaFold2 — colouring the result by domain: Cex (exoglucanase, green),
            the carbohydrate-binding module that holds the two halves together (blue), and CenA
            (endoglucanase, pink), joined by flexible proline/threonine-rich linkers.
          </p>

          <div class="key-finding">
            <div class="key-finding-label">High confidence, within limits</div>
            <div class="key-finding-value">pLDDT 89.3 (Cex) · 93.5 (CenA)</div>
            <div class="key-finding-context">
              Both domains individually fold with high confidence. The catch: the inter-domain
              confidence is low (pTM 0.5; mean predicted alignment error 26.6 Å) — the linkers are
              floppy enough in real life that AlphaFold can't pin down how the two domains sit
              relative to each other. We trust the shape of each domain on its own, not the overall
              "pose" of the whole fusion protein.
            </div>
          </div>

          <details class="expand">
            <summary>Why does a flexible linker make the overall shape unreliable?</summary>
            <div class="expand-body">
              <p>
                AlphaFold predicts confidence per residue (pLDDT) and per residue-pair (predicted
                alignment error, PAE). A folded domain's internal geometry is usually well
                constrained — there's only one way the chain can pack into a stable fold — so
                pLDDT is high throughout it. A flexible linker has no single correct conformation;
                it can be in many different positions at different moments, so the model can't
                confidently place the two domains it connects relative to each other, even if it's
                very confident about each domain individually. This is expected biology, not a
                modelling failure — treat the two domains as independently reliable, and the
                relative orientation between them as unknown.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── DL-R2: Cex validation ─── -->
      <div class="result-block" id="drylab-cex">
        <div class="result-header">
          <div class="result-id">DL-R2</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Checking Cex's active site against a real crystal structure</h3>
            <div class="result-meta">
              <span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span>
              <span class="result-method">Methods: <a href={`${b}/methods#drylab`}>DL-2</a></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-body">
          <p>
            Unlike the fusion as a whole, Cex alone has a published crystal structure to check
            against: 1FHD, a Cex-family enzyme solved with a bound inhibitor
            <a class="cite" href="#ref-notenboom98">[1]</a>. We superimposed our predicted Cex
            structure onto it to see how well the model's active site matches reality.
          </p>

          <div class="key-finding">
            <div class="key-finding-label">Key Result</div>
            <div class="key-finding-value">RMSD = <strong>0.293 Å</strong></div>
            <div class="key-finding-context">
              Cα RMSD over 1,958 aligned atoms against the 1FHD crystal structure. This is within
              the 0.3–0.4 Å range the original crystallographers report for two different crystal
              forms of Cex compared to <em>each other</em> — in other words, our predicted model
              disagrees with the real structure by no more than two real structures disagree with
              one another. The active-site geometry, including the catalytic residues GLU127 and
              GLU233, is reproduced faithfully.
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Interactive 3D structural overlay — kept from the earlier Mol* viewer -->
          <div class="viewer-embed">
            <iframe
              src={`${b}/molstar-test`}
              class="mol-iframe"
              loading="lazy"
              title="Interactive 3D: Cex AlphaFold model vs 1FHD crystal structure overlay"
            ></iframe>
          </div>
          <p class="viewer-caption">
            Interactive 3D overlay: predicted Cex structure (teal) superimposed on the 1FHD crystal
            structure (orange) — Cα RMSD 0.579 Å over 311 matched pairs in this particular alignment run.
            Yellow spheres mark GLN87, GLU127 and GLU233. Drag to rotate; use the toggle buttons to
            view each structure independently.
          </p>

          <div class="callout">
            <p class="callout-title">Why this step matters</p>
            <p>
              Every docking result later in this page depends on the predicted structure being
              trustworthy. This validation is what lets us treat the Cex model as a stand-in for
              the real enzyme when testing how it interacts with sugars computationally.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── DL-R3: Cex mutation target screening ─── -->
      <div class="result-block" id="drylab-cex-targets">
        <div class="result-header">
          <div class="result-id">DL-R3</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Does Cex already have a little latent β-glucosidase activity — and where would you nudge it?</h3>
            <div class="result-meta">
              <span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span>
              <span class="result-method">Methods: <a href={`${b}/methods#drylab`}>DL-3, DL-4, DL-5</a></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-body">
          <p>
            Cex's fold resembles known β-glucosidase enzymes closely enough that we asked a
            direct question: can cellobiose — the two-sugar fragment a true β-glucosidase would
            split into glucose — fit into Cex's existing active site at all? We docked cellobiose
            into the validated Cex model with AutoDock Vina and analysed the predicted contacts
            with PLIP.
          </p>

          <div class="key-finding">
            <div class="key-finding-label">Top docking pose</div>
            <div class="key-finding-value">Sits right next to the existing catalytic residues</div>
            <div class="key-finding-context">
              The best-scoring pose places cellobiose in hydrogen-bonding distance of GLU127 and
              GLU233 — the same two residues Cex already uses to cut its natural substrate. That's
              consistent with the idea that Cex could be nudged toward glucosidase-like chemistry
              without needing a whole new active site built from scratch.
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Multiple-sequence alignment against 15 homologous GH10 enzymes, cross-checked with
            ConSurf conservation scoring, confirmed that the catalytic motifs around GLU127 (an
            unusual "NEA" variant of the normal "NEP" motif) and GLU233 (the "ITELD" motif) are
            essentially universal across the family — strong evidence that both residues are
            functionally essential, not incidental.
          </p>

          <div class="fig-single">
            <img src={`${b}/images/results/gh10-consurf-motifs.jpg`} alt="Cex structure coloured by evolutionary conservation (ConSurf), with the NEA and TELD catalytic motifs boxed in green" loading="lazy" />
            <p class="fig-caption">Cex coloured by ConSurf conservation (red = most conserved). The NEA and TELD boxes mark the two catalytic motifs — both are deep red, i.e. essentially invariant across 15 homologous enzymes.</p>
          </div>

          <h4>Where would you aim mutagenesis, if you were targeting it?</h4>
          <p>
            HotSpot Wizard flagged candidate residues near the active site that are mutable
            without obviously destroying the protein's fold. Those candidates were then screened
            computationally with FoldX (for stability) and re-docked with cellobiose (for binding).
            The single most promising candidate to emerge was <strong>Asn44 → Trp</strong> (ΔΔG
            −3.2 kcal/mol, i.e. more stable than wild type, not less).
          </p>

          <details class="expand">
            <summary>Full ranked list of computational candidates (top 10 by predicted stability)</summary>
            <div class="expand-body">
              <div class="table-wrap"><div class="table-scroll"><table class="data-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Mutation</th><th>ΔΔG (kcal/mol)</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>1</td><td class="mono">N44W</td><td>−3.215</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>2</td><td class="mono">E43R</td><td>−2.177</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>3</td><td class="mono">S86R</td><td>−1.864</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>4</td><td class="mono">N44Y</td><td>−1.788</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>5</td><td class="mono">N44F</td><td>−1.719</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>6</td><td class="mono">E43L</td><td>−0.797</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>7</td><td class="mono">T81G</td><td>−0.783</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>8</td><td class="mono">K47R</td><td>−0.745</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>9</td><td class="mono">E43M</td><td>−0.684</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>10</td><td class="mono">S86D</td><td>−0.637</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table></div></div>
              <p class="table-note">Negative ΔΔG = predicted <em>more</em> stable than wild type. Full ranked list of 25 candidates and per-mutation docking scores available in the project's supplementary data. See the <a href={`${b}/notebook/2026-06-25-dry`}>25 June lab notebook entry</a> for the full FoldX and re-docking workflow.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── DL-R4: CenA / Project B ─── -->
      <div class="result-block" id="drylab-cena">
        <div class="result-header">
          <div class="result-id">DL-R4</div>
          <div>
            <h3>CenA, the other half: where could the existing activity be improved?</h3>
            <div class="result-meta">
              <span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span>
              <span class="result-method">Methods: <a href={`${b}/methods#drylab`}>DL-3, DL-4, DL-5</a></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-body">
          <p>
            CenA (the endoglucanase half of CxnA, family GH6) already works — the Project B goal
            isn't to invent a new activity but to make an existing one better. We docked
            cellotetraose (a four-sugar cellulose fragment) into CenA's active site and confirmed
            contacts with its three catalytic aspartates: Asp595 (acid catalyst), Asp631
            (stabilises the acid catalyst's protonation state) and Asp771 (base catalyst).
          </p>

          <div class="fig-single">
            <img src={`${b}/images/results/cena-consurf.jpg`} alt="CenA structure coloured by evolutionary conservation (ConSurf)" loading="lazy" />
            <p class="fig-caption">CenA coloured by ConSurf conservation across 41 homologous GH6 sequences (magenta = most conserved, teal = most variable). 13 of the 27 residues contacting the docked substrate are fully conserved — a strong signal of functional importance.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            As with Cex, candidate mutations were screened by combining FoldX stability
            predictions with re-docking scores — this time checking both metrics together, plus
            whether the catalytic geometry held up across repeated modelling runs.
          </p>

          <div class="key-finding">
            <div class="key-finding-label">Best overall candidate</div>
            <div class="key-finding-value">K671S</div>
            <div class="key-finding-context">
              Lysine 671 → serine showed the largest improvement in predicted substrate binding
              (ΔVina score −0.85 kcal/mol better than wild type) while passing the catalytic
              geometry check in every modelled run.
            </div>
          </div>

          <details class="expand">
            <summary>Full CenA mutation screen (stability + docking + geometry check)</summary>
            <div class="expand-body">
              <div class="table-wrap"><div class="table-scroll"><table class="data-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Mutation</th><th>ΔΔG (kcal/mol)</th><th>Mean Vina score</th><th>ΔVina score</th><th>Catalytic geometry</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td class="mono">WT</td><td>0.00</td><td>−7.83</td><td>reference</td><td>Pass</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">T697V</td><td>−1.75</td><td>−7.46</td><td>+0.37</td><td>Partial</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">T583I</td><td>−1.52</td><td>−8.12</td><td>−0.29</td><td>Pass</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">T583V</td><td>−1.24</td><td>−7.52</td><td>+0.31</td><td>Pass</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">S520G</td><td>−0.98</td><td>−7.83</td><td>0.00</td><td>Partial</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">S598Q</td><td>−0.36</td><td>−8.18</td><td>−0.35</td><td>Pass</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">S598N</td><td>−0.07</td><td>−8.09</td><td>−0.26</td><td>Pass</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">K671A</td><td>0.22</td><td>−8.10</td><td>−0.26</td><td>Pass</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">K671G</td><td>0.27</td><td>−8.00</td><td>−0.16</td><td>Partial</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono"><strong>K671S</strong></td><td>0.30</td><td><strong>−8.68</strong></td><td><strong>−0.85</strong></td><td>Pass</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">Q635M</td><td>0.38</td><td>−7.44</td><td>+0.39</td><td>Pass</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table></div></div>
              <p class="table-note">ΔΔG: FoldX-predicted stability change (negative = more stable). Vina score: predicted binding free energy with cellotetraose (more negative = tighter predicted binding). K671S has a slightly destabilising ΔΔG but the best binding improvement of any candidate that keeps correct catalytic geometry — a stability/activity trade-off, not a free win.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── DL-R5: Bridge to wet lab ─── -->
      <div class="result-block" id="drylab-bridge">
        <div class="result-header">
          <div class="result-id">DL-R5</div>
          <div>
            <h3>An important honesty note: prediction guided priorities, it didn't dictate the mutants</h3>
            <div class="result-meta">
              <span class="badge badge--neutral">Context</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-body">
          <div class="callout callout--warn">
            <p class="callout-title">Read this before the wet-lab results</p>
            <p>
              N44W and K671S are the dry lab's top <em>computational</em> candidates — but the
              actual wet-lab mutant library wasn't built by inserting those specific changes.
              It was built with error-prone PCR, which introduces mutations <strong>randomly</strong>
              across the gene. The computational work told us the Cex and CenA active sites were
              worth targeting and gave us a reference point for interpreting what random
              mutagenesis turned up — it didn't guarantee any particular mutation would actually
              appear in the library. Keep that distinction in mind in the section below: none of
              the sequenced wet-lab mutants carry N44W or K671S.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- ═══ WET LAB RESULTS ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <section class="section section--alt" id="wetlab" aria-labelledby="wetlab-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-header">
        <p class="eyebrow">Experimental results</p>
        <h2 id="wetlab-heading">Wet Lab — building and screening real mutants</h2>
        <p>
          Three screens, run in sequence, each narrowing down which of the random mutants were
          worth a closer look: a visual test for endoglucanase activity, a colour test for
          glucosidase activity, and finally a quantitative fluorescence assay we had to build and
          validate from scratch.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- WL-R1: Plasmid -->
      <div class="result-block" id="wetlab-plasmid">
        <div class="result-header">
          <div class="result-id">WL-R1</div>
          <div>
            <h3>The plasmid, and an uninvited stowaway</h3>
            <div class="result-meta">
              <span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span>
              <span class="result-method">Methods: <a href={`${b}/methods#wetlab`}>WL-1, WL-2</a></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-body">
          <p>
            CxnA was carried on a pSB1C3 plasmid backbone with a lac promoter and ribosome
            binding site ahead of the insert. Transforming <em>E. coli</em> with this construct
            retained both exo- and endo-glucanase activity, confirming the starting enzyme was
            functional before any mutagenesis began.
          </p>

          <div class="fig-single">
            <img src={`${b}/images/results/plasmid-map.png`} alt="Plasmid map of the CxnA construct, showing the Cex (GH10) and CenA (GH6) domains, restriction sites, and the duplicated chloramphenicol-resistance region" loading="lazy" />
            <p class="fig-caption">Plasmid map of the CxnA construct. CxnA itself is only two domains — Cex (exoglucanase) and CenA (endoglucanase) — joined through a carbohydrate-binding module (CBD2). The duplicated chloramphenicol-resistance region on the right was an unplanned discovery (below).</p>
          </div>

          <div class="key-finding key-finding--warn">
            <div class="key-finding-label">Unplanned finding</div>
            <div class="key-finding-value">An extra ~1 kb of DNA in the backbone</div>
            <div class="key-finding-context">
              Gel electrophoresis and sequencing turned up an insert in the backbone that wasn't
              supposed to be there. A BLASTp search against the translated sequence matched it to
              a duplicated chloramphenicol-resistance pseudogene — most likely a quirk of this
              specific plasmid stock rather than anything we introduced. It doesn't appear to have
              blocked cloning or expression, but its effect on plasmid stability wasn't formally
              tested.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- WL-R2: Library generation -->
      <div class="result-block" id="wetlab-library">
        <div class="result-header">
          <div class="result-id">WL-R2</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Building the mutant library — smaller than planned</h3>
            <div class="result-meta">
              <span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span>
              <span class="result-method">Methods: <a href={`${b}/methods#wetlab`}>WL-3</a></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-body">
          <p>
            Error-prone PCR (epPCR) was applied only to the CxnA insert, using manganese chloride
            to deliberately lower the copying polymerase's accuracy — the backbone, promoter and
            ribosome binding site were amplified separately with a high-fidelity polymerase so
            mutations wouldn't land somewhere that would confound the activity readout. We ran
            this on two versions of the gene in parallel: the native sequence, and a
            codon-optimised version ordered as synthetic DNA blocks (to work around the gene's
            unusually high, amplification-hostile GC content — 71%).
          </p>

          <div class="key-finding key-finding--warn">
            <div class="key-finding-label">Honest limitation</div>
            <div class="key-finding-value">24 (codon-optimised) and 15 (native) colonies</div>
            <div class="key-finding-context">
              Both libraries came out markedly smaller than planned — the GC content made PCR
              temperamental, and several technical issues (inconsistent mixing, an
              unexpectedly-migrating linearised plasmid control) slowed troubleshooting. A small
              library means fewer chances for a rare beneficial mutation to be sampled at all;
              this is the single biggest constraint on everything that follows.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- WL-R3: Congo Red -->
      <div class="result-block" id="wetlab-congored">
        <div class="result-header">
          <div class="result-id">WL-R3</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Screen 1 — Congo red: did endoglucanase activity survive?</h3>
            <div class="result-meta">
              <span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span>
              <span class="result-method">Methods: <a href={`${b}/methods#wetlab`}>WL-4</a></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-body">
          <p>
            Congo red dye binds intact cellulose (here, the related polymer CMC) but washes off
            wherever an active endoglucanase has chewed it up — so a colony with working CenA
            activity shows up as a clear halo on a red background. This was run first, as a cheap
            way to immediately discard any mutant that had simply broken the protein.
          </p>

          <div class="fig-single">
            <img src={`${b}/images/results/congo-red-mutants.jpg`} alt="Congo red assay plates for codon-optimised and native CxnA mutant colonies, showing clearing halos around most colonies" loading="lazy" />
            <p class="fig-caption">Congo red screen. (A) All 15 codon-optimised mutants, numbered. (B) All 15 native-sequence mutants (only 15 total colonies were available in that library). A clear halo = endoglucanase activity retained.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="key-finding">
            <div class="key-finding-label">Result</div>
            <div class="key-finding-value">7 of 15 native, 15 of 15 codon-optimised retained a halo</div>
            <div class="key-finding-context">
              All 15 codon-optimised mutants showed some clearing, at varying sizes. Of the native
              mutants, colony 4 had the largest halo (closest to wild type); colonies 12, 14 and 15
              showed <strong>no</strong> clearing at all — a likely sign that their mutation broke
              the protein outright, not a subtle activity change. All five of these were flagged
              for sequencing.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- WL-R4: X-gluc -->
      <div class="result-block" id="wetlab-xgluc">
        <div class="result-header">
          <div class="result-id">WL-R4</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Screen 2 — X-gluc: any sign of new glucosidase activity?</h3>
            <div class="result-meta">
              <span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span>
              <span class="result-method">Methods: <a href={`${b}/methods#wetlab`}>WL-5</a></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-body">
          <p>
            X-gluc is a colourless compound that turns a colony visibly blue if the cell is
            producing β-glucosidase-like activity. Every mutant from both libraries — 30 colonies
            total — was screened this way in parallel with Congo red, alongside controls: a known
            β-glucosidase (positive control), wild-type CxnA, and a non-functional His-tagged
            CxnA (negative control).
          </p>

          <div class="fig-single">
            <img src={`${b}/images/results/screen-controls.jpg`} alt="Control plates for the Congo red (left) and X-gluc (right) screens, comparing a known beta-glucosidase, wild-type CxnA, and a non-functional His-tagged CxnA" loading="lazy" />
            <p class="fig-caption">Controls for both screens. Left: Congo red. Right: X-gluc — note that wild-type CxnA (top right spot) already shows a faint blue tint, not just the positive-control β-glucosidase (bottom left).</p>
          </div>

          <div class="fig-single">
            <img src={`${b}/images/results/xgluc-mutants.jpg`} alt="X-gluc assay plates for codon-optimised and native CxnA mutant colonies" loading="lazy" />
            <p class="fig-caption">X-gluc screen on all 30 mutants. (A) codon-optimised. (B) native sequence.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="key-finding key-finding--warn">
            <div class="key-finding-label">Unexpected background signal</div>
            <div class="key-finding-value">Wild-type CxnA already shows faint baseline activity</div>
            <div class="key-finding-context">
              The non-functional His-tagged negative control looked the same as wild-type CxnA —
              both showed a faint blue tint beyond the truly blank background. This matches the
              dry-lab prediction (DL-R3) that Cex might already have some latent capacity to engage
              cellobiose-like substrates. No mutant came close to the true positive control, but
              several (native colonies 4, 14, 15; codon-optimised colonies 2, 4, 7, 12) were darker
              than the wild-type baseline and were flagged for sequencing and quantitative
              follow-up.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- WL-R5: MUC assay -->
      <div class="result-block" id="wetlab-muc">
        <div class="result-header">
          <div class="result-id">WL-R5</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Screen 3 — putting a number on it: the MUC fluorescence assay</h3>
            <div class="result-meta">
              <span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span>
              <span class="result-method">Methods: <a href={`${b}/methods#wetlab`}>WL-6</a> · Full troubleshooting: <a href={`${b}/notebook`}>Lab Notebook, Aug–Sep</a></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-body">
          <p>
            Congo red and X-gluc are useful for sorting mutants into rough buckets, but neither
            gives a number you can actually compare between colonies. To measure exoglucanase
            activity precisely, we used MUC — a substrate that releases a fluorescent molecule
            (4-MU) in proportion to how much active enzyme is present. Getting trustworthy numbers
            out of this assay took six rounds of calibration (the full story — including a
            substrate mix-up that briefly looked like a real signal — is in the lab notebook);
            the validated version is summarised below.
          </p>

          <div class="fig-single">
            <img src={`${b}/images/results/muc-assay-establishment.jpg`} alt="Four-panel chart establishing the MUC fluorescence assay: calibration curve, volume-dependence artefact, enzyme-titration linearity, and background controls" loading="lazy" />
            <p class="fig-caption">Establishing a trustworthy assay. (A) Fluorescence is only proportional to 4-MU concentration below 6.25 µM — above that, the reading undershoots for optical reasons, not biology, so all rates were fit only within the reliable range. (B) Adding more lysate gave progressively less signal per microlitre — a volume artefact, not reduced activity. (C) Fixed by titrating active lysate against inactive (frameshift) lysate instead, which restored a straight-line relationship at two different temperatures/settings. (D) No signal from substrate, medium, or host alone — the frameshift control sits at just 0.7% of induced CxnA.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            With the assay validated, we quantified exoglucanase activity for the seven mutants
            flagged by the earlier screens, each compared against a wild-type control grown and
            lysed alongside it on the same day.
          </p>

          <div class="fig-single">
            <img src={`${b}/images/results/muc-quant-screen.jpg`} alt="Bar chart of exoglucanase activity for seven epPCR mutants and wild-type controls, relative to wild type, with a 3-sigma detection threshold band" loading="lazy" />
            <p class="fig-caption">Activity of all seven flagged mutants, relative to a same-day wild-type control (bars = median of 3 technical replicates; whiskers = range). The shaded band marks how much two colonies of the <em>same, unmutated</em> genotype normally differ from each other just from day-to-day prep variation — only a bar that clears this band by itself is a plausible real effect.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="key-finding">
            <div class="key-finding-label">The headline number</div>
            <div class="key-finding-value">9.5% colony-to-colony noise → need ≥1.40× to call it real</div>
            <div class="key-finding-context">
              Before trusting any "improved" mutant, we first measured how much two colonies of
              the identical, unmutated wild type differ from each other purely from everyday
              variability in growth and lysis. The answer was 9.5%. Statistically, that means a
              single mutant colony needs to beat wild type by at least 1.40× before we can call the
              difference real rather than noise (a "3σ" threshold, in the jargon).
            </div>
          </div>

          <details class="expand">
            <summary>How each of the 7 flagged mutants actually scored</summary>
            <div class="expand-body">
              <div class="table-wrap"><div class="table-scroll"><table class="data-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Mutant</th><th>Activity vs wild type</th><th>Call</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td class="mono"><strong>CO12</strong></td><td>1.39× and 1.31× (two independent colonies, mean 1.35×)</td><td><strong>Candidate, not confirmed</strong> — above the single-colony threshold's ballpark but not past it with statistical confidence (p = 0.11)</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">CO2</td><td>0.72×</td><td>At or below wild type</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">CO4</td><td>0.99×</td><td>Indistinguishable from wild type</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">CO7</td><td>0.57×</td><td>Below wild type</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">T4</td><td>0.16×</td><td>Strongly attenuated</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">T14</td><td>≈ 0× (indistinguishable from the dead-enzyme control)</td><td>Functionally dead</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class="mono">T15</td><td>≈ 0× (indistinguishable from the dead-enzyme control)</td><td>Functionally dead</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table></div></div>
              <p class="table-note">WT-CO vs WT-T themselves agreed to within 1.1% of each other, confirming codon optimisation alone doesn't change activity — differences above are attributable to the epPCR mutations (or to prep noise, for anything inside the shaded band).</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- WL-R6: Sequencing -->
      <div class="result-block" id="wetlab-sequencing">
        <div class="result-header">
          <div class="result-id">WL-R6</div>
          <div>
            <h3>What mutations were actually there?</h3>
            <div class="result-meta">
              <span class="badge badge--confirmed">Confirmed</span>
              <span class="result-method">Methods: <a href={`${b}/methods#wetlab`}>WL-7</a></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-body">
          <p>
            Six colonies flagged by the screens above were miniprepped and sent for Sanger
            sequencing to find out what had actually changed in their DNA.
          </p>

          <div class="key-finding key-finding--warn">
            <div class="key-finding-label">A sobering finding</div>
            <div class="key-finding-value">Most sequenced colonies carried no mutations at all</div>
            <div class="key-finding-context">
              Several of the sequenced codon-optimised colonies came back identical to the parent
              sequence — epPCR simply hadn't introduced a change in those particular colonies by
              chance. That's actually informative: if "mutants" with zero genetic change still show
              a 0.57–1.39× spread in measured activity, that spread has to be prep-to-prep noise,
              not genetics — which is exactly the kind of variation the 1.40× statistical threshold
              above was designed to filter out.
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The clearest exception was colony T4 (the one that showed strongly attenuated activity,
            0.16× wild type). Its sequencing surfaced two point mutations: a glycine → aspartic
            acid change in the exoglucanase (Cex) domain, and an aspartic acid → glycine change in
            the endoglucanase (CenA) domain. Because CxnA's two active sites sit several hundred
            residues apart, losing <em>both</em> activities from two separate, unrelated point
            mutations landing simultaneously is a coincidence we'd rather not have to explain — a
            single misfolding event destabilising the whole fusion protein is the simpler
            explanation, and fits the data better.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- WL-R7: Verdict -->
      <div class="result-block" id="wetlab-verdict">
        <div class="result-header">
          <div class="result-id">WL-R7</div>
          <div>
            <h3>So — did it work?</h3>
            <div class="result-meta">
              <span class="badge badge--neutral">The honest answer</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-body">
          <p>
            <strong>No mutant demonstrated a confirmed improvement in activity.</strong> CO12 is a
            genuine candidate for a future round — it's the only variant that beat wild type at
            all, and its 1.35× average sits right at the edge of our detection limit — but it
            doesn't clear the bar for statistical confidence yet, and more replicates are needed
            before calling it a real hit rather than a lucky draw. Two mutants (T14, T15) appear to
            have lost function entirely, most likely through protein misfolding rather than a
            targeted change to either active site.
          </p>
          <p>
            What <em>did</em> work: we built a complete, repeatable pipeline — random mutagenesis,
            two fast qualitative screens, a validated quantitative fluorescence assay, and
            sequencing to close the loop — that took a library from "30 random colonies" down to
            "one statistically-interesting candidate" in a single round. The library was small and
            the GC-rich gene was a genuine technical obstacle throughout; a larger library run
            through the same pipeline is the obvious next step, not a different approach.
          </p>

          <div class="callout">
            <p class="callout-title">Why we're reporting a null result at all</p>
            <p>
              A "nothing improved, but here's exactly why and how confident we are" result is a
              real scientific outcome, not a failure to hide. Every negative or inconclusive finding
              on this page — the collapsed T14/T15 mutants, the unconfirmed CO12 candidate, the
              small library sizes — is reported as measured, not rounded up or omitted.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

